<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caspe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caspe is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in Aragon, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro River</a>. It is best known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> of 1412, which chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a> as king of the Crown of Aragon. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it was the seat of the anarchist Regional Defence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-aragon">Council of Aragon</a> from late 1936 to 1937.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caspe.jpg" alt="Caspe" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ecelan · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caspe_-_Colegiata_de_Sta_Mar%C3%ADa_la_Mayor_-_Fachada03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Caspe lies 104 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> on the banks of the Guadalope river, a few kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, in one of the most arid areas of Aragon. A popular belief derives the name from ancient inhabitants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, but this lacks philological support. The name appears in Andalusi sources as Qsp, Qasp or Qasb, and may be related to an Arabic word or to an Indo-European root meaning oak.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was among the last of Lower Aragon to be settled, although a biface of 150,000 to 100,000 years ago shows the passage of Neanderthal hunter-gatherers. Towns and tumular necropolises of the Celtic Hallstatt culture appeared around the 8th century BCE. From 713 to the Christian reconquest in the first half of the 12th century the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> formed the northernmost mark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-aragon">Alfonso II of Aragon</a> took Caspe between June and September 1169, in a campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnau-mir-de-tost">Arnau Mir de Tost</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a>. Until 1610 Christian and Muslim communities lived in the town, each with its own municipal area.</p><p>Because of its position Caspe suffered in many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s conflicts from the 17th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> it supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> cause while its neighbours chose the Austrian claimant. French troops occupied it from June 1809 to 1813, and the local lawyer Agustín de Quinto, who collaborated with them, was named commissar of the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, which made Caspe capital of the lower half of Aragon. In June 1813 Colonel Ramón Gayán retook it after a siege of fifteen days, using tunnels to blow up part of the castle where the French were barricaded.</p><p>The Statute of Autonomy of Aragon of 1936, also called the Statute of Caspe, was drafted in the town. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> it was the seat of the anarchist Regional Defense Council of Aragon, which a military intervention dissolved in August 1937, when the 11th Division under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enrique-lister">Enrique Líster</a> arrested its president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquin-ascaso">Joaquín Ascaso</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Caspe is the seat of the comarca of Bajo Aragón-Caspe, and its municipality of 503 square kilometres is the fourth largest in Aragon. Its heritage includes the Plano del Pulido cave paintings, declared a World Heritage Site in 1998, the Roman Miralpeix Mausoleum and the remains of the Castle of the Commitment, built by the knights of the Hospital Order.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caspe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1936 the CNT created the Regional Defence Council of Aragon, first at Fraga and then at <b>Caspe</b>, under Joaquín Ascaso.</blockquote><a href="/state/regional-defence-council-of-aragon">Regional Defence Council of Aragon</a></li></ul></section>
